 when we  do factor analysis on this and ask how  many latent variables do we need to  explain a hundred percent of the  variability lo and behold if we have uh  uh one latent variable we get roughly 50  percent of the variability if we have  two latent variables we get 100 of the  variability in our model so uh this is  not a good representation for what's  going on here i'll remind you that uh  the data showed that the variability  wasn't well explained by two dimensions  but really on average four dimensions  here so this simple weakly coupled uh  model of pfc isn't going to cut it and  if we wanted to have higher if you  wanted to have a higher dimensional  activity in php it would need to come  from more outside sources our hypothesis  one that i'm not tracking here i want to  explain it with internal dynamics in pfc  so what are we going to do here well  let's make the coupling between cells  larger so let's take this one over k  coupling and turn it into what's uh one  over square root k coupling this is a  standard scaling that's familiar to  people that work in balanced network  theory and what balanced network theory  means is that the excitatory coupling is  fairly large one over square root k is  much much larger than one over k  uh and because recurrent excitation is  now quite impressive and quite large it  needs to be counteracted and balanced by  uh recurrent inhibition in the system  here  so we need to have excitatory inhibitory  cells to make sure that the system is  stable despite this large uh recurrent  coupling here so now we include  inhibitory cells we give them a right in  the left side projection from v4 as well  and when we do simulations for this  network we get really different dynamics  uh if we just do it as is here we get  bi-stable dynamics so that the the left  neurons uh which are on the top here  when they're firing vigorously they're  actively suppressing neurons that prefer  the right uh v4 and then vice versa you  get this bi-stable switching dynamics  that occur in this network a really  non-linear property this would not be  captured by a simple linear system uh  there's really two  two solutions here battling it out  um  this doesn't uh this when we look at  correlation structure we get that  neurons within the same uh preferring  the same  v4 input they tend to be positively  correlated neurons that prefer opposite  v4 inputs are negatively correlated and  this has been well described in papers  previously from my research group  this uh unfortunately doesn't exactly  look like the data while neurons that  prefer the same visual hemifield uh are  positively correlated that's in the the  blue and the and the cyan uh that's not  the case for neurons that uh one neuron  that prefers uh the left and when  they're not prefers right they're weakly  anti-correlated but not strongly  anti-correlate  we can break this uh this strong  anti-correlation by now taking our  network and coupling neurons in an  attractor framework here where neurons  that like the same and like the same  visual input tend to wire with one  another uh with stronger connections  here so this is this clustering  coefficient so on the left clustering  coefficient of one would mean that  there's no structure whatsoever in pfc  clustering coefficient of 2.4 means that  neurons that like the same uh hemifield  input  uh  tend to coupled with one another with a  strength of 2.4 that of the of the of  the opposite coupling here so  when we put this clustering into our  system we can get rid of this sort of  pathologic bistability that doesn't  really describe our network that well  here uh and that's what we see here if  the system becomes uh less and less uh  by stable as we put in higher and higher  clustering and again this is work that  has been explained in previous papers in  my lab  when we look at the dimensionality this  is new though no one's seen this when we  look at the dimensionality of our  network when our clustering is quite uh  when there's no clustering in our  network the dimensionality in the model  is uh still quite low that's the c  equals one solution as we put clustering  the model the the overall dimensionality  that we would pick up from factor  analysis grows uh and starts to match  what we saw in the data here reported so  this is somewhat satisfying and putting  uh attractor like networks in pfc is  relatively standard now we do think of  pfc as as a network that is tuned for  specific inputs  so let's go a little farther with this  so now that we can uh we have a model  that replicates the data in pfc  reasonably well let's sort of open the  hood of the car here and understand a  little bit more what we can do is we can  take the dynamics of our model and uh  segregate periods of time when the model  is such that the left uh  network is dominant over the right  that's  zones on the raster plot or periods of  time where the right is dominant over  the left that is in the orange periods  of time so we can segregate our network  into being into one of two states and we  these intermediate transition zones  we're going to throw them out of our  data set right now and not analyze them  here  so  when we restrict our network to being in  one of the two states in this particular  case the left state so that means  neurons that prefer the left input are  highly active and we do our our linear  theory where we estimate the gains and  we take our linear response functions we  do a pretty decent job at explaining the  covariability uh in the network um the  y-axis is the theory we would get from  fitting the the gains and using that  matrix formula there versus what we  measure from the simulations on the on  the the x-axis here and there's a pretty  decent job at explaining everything  despite the fact that the coupling is  fairly strong here remember this is the  strongly coupled network interactions  are quite impressive here but we  restrict our system to being in one of  in the state uh l here we get a pretty  decent job  if we restrict our system to being in  the state where the right the the right  uh pfc neurons are dominant over the  left we also get a pretty decent uh uh  match between theory and sense  um however if we look at the gain  function  we see that if we linearize our system  where the neurons are in the left state  versus the right state the gain does  change here so those gi's uh are  different depending on which state we  are in that's again a hallmark of a  nonlinear system so the the gain or the  slope of the transfer function of a  neuron changes depending upon which  state it's in effect sampling the  non-linearity of that neurons transfer  function and that's true for for all of  our neurons here  so  here this is sort of the last couple  slides here if we do our uh  dimensionality analysis and we ask  what's the uh cumulative variance uh  explained shared variance explained how  many dimensions do we need to explain 95  98 99 of the variance if we restrict our  system to being in one of the two states  either state r or state l it looks like  we just need two dimensions and that  makes sense from our linear theory  before there's two v4 inputs they're  both one-dimensional pfc is receiving  both of them if it's acting like a  linear system then it'll just inherit  the dimensionality of those v4 inputs  and you get the answer too  however if we look at the system  for the full dynamics where we're  allowing the transitions to happen  now in order to explain a high  percentage of the variability we need a  higher demand we need we need three or  three or more dimensions here and this  is because these two dimensional  manifolds given by the linearizations  are different depending on when you're  in whether you're in state r or state l  and the network is transitioning between  these and if you build a linear theory  that stitches these two things together  you can do a better job at this and this  is effectively saying well when you look  at the full system there's two  dimensional variability uh when the  system's in state are there's another  manifold which is two-dimensional when  the system is in state l and sort of the  the union of these two manifolds it's  effectively four dimensions here this is  sort of not not a proof in any sense but  sort of a uh a good way to think about  why variability the dimension of  variability is expanding when we go from  v4 to pfc it's the non-linearities in  pfc  that uh sort of sample uh different  regions of activity space here and uh  and amplify them  for inputs here  so some of the takeaways here from this  is that visual area v4 has very low  dimensionality while pfc shows a higher  dimensionality this expansion be from v4  to pfc in terms of dimensionality can be  captured if the pfc networks are  strongly coupled and they have a tractor  structure with their connectivity and we  can get a good understanding this of  this dimensionality expansion by  stitching together linearizations of the  network about distinct states here so  this is the beginning of a path that i'm  taking with my lab and my collaborators  to understand how  the dimensions of activity are are  transmitted across across cortical  networks as we start recording from more  and more regions of the brain  simultaneously these kinds of theories  will be more and more important to see  here uh my last slide is to give  acknowledgements here so really the the  the work uh majority of the analysis and  modeling work was all done by danielle  rager um who was a graduate student in  my lab now she works for ibm and her  neural norfolk chip division  uh and this is a collaboration with matt  smith as well as byron yu  and it really comes out from a brain  initiative award from the theories  models and methods for analysis of  complex data from the brain uh this has  been a great mechanism for me to receive  my lab and my collaborators to receive  funding